  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Ga-Adangbe\"\nsubtitle: \"Ghana\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | Ga-Adangbe people |\n| Where | Ghana |\n| How many | Not provided |\n| Language | Not documented |\n| Religion | Not documented |\n| Known for | A folded embroidered cap · Wooden coffins and model coffins · A wooden fertility figurine |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe sources give little information about the Ga-Adangbe people. The catalogue records a cap, several wooden coffins, model coffins, and a fertility figurine. These objects are not clearly identified as specifically Ga-Adangbe forms.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe recorded material culture consists mainly of one embroidered cap and several carved or painted wooden objects.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nThe catalogue records one folded, embroidered cotton cap.\n\n- **Four-panel embroidered cap** — This cotton cap folds flat in four panels and has yellow and pink embroidery on purple cloth, a white lower border, and a red and white button at the top.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nThe museum records several carved wooden coffins and model coffins in unusual forms.\n\n- **Eagle coffin** — The eagle coffin has painted feather markings, hinged wings, carrying handles, and a textile-lined cavity.\n- **Elephant coffin** — The elephant coffin has a cavity lined with green silk and red textile.\n- **Catfish coffin** — The painted catfish coffin has a hinged wooden lid over its cavity.\n- **Cocoa-pod coffin** — The painted cocoa-pod coffin has a hinged wooden lid over its cavity.\n- **Lager-bottle coffin** — The painted lager-bottle coffin is marked “Premium Club” and has a hinged wooden lid over its cavity.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nThe sources do not document Ga-Adangbe jewelry or body-adornment traditions, but one catalogue record describes a decorated wooden figurine.\n\n- **Wooden fertility figurine** — This standing male figurine has a blue bead girdle and necklace, another necklace, straw around the neck, and partial white paint.\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- British Museum catalogue records for the cap, coffins, model coffins, and wooden figure described in the supplied source material.\n- No Wikipedia article was supplied among the sources used.\n- No UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage inscription was supplied; the source material states that there are no UNESCO ICH inscriptions for this country.\n",
